-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-- Saturated pixels (clipped at 0 or 1) are masked out during least squares solving to prevent distortion [S1, unofficial].
-- The mask is updated iteratively based on the current corrected estimate, which helps handle non-linear transformations [S1, unofficial].
-- Precision issues may arise if the design matrix `a_mat` is ill-conditioned. Using `rcond` in least squares helps [S1, unofficial].
-
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: $O(\text{num\_iters} \cdot N \cdot K^2)$ or $O(\text{num\_iters} \cdot N \cdot C^3)$ depending on implementation, where $K \approx C^2/2$. Solving least squares for each channel per iteration dominates.
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N \cdot K)$ for storing the design matrix.
-- **Strengths**: Handles non-linear color mappings; robust to clipping by masking saturated pixels; iterative refinement improves accuracy.
-- **Limitations**: Computationally expensive for large images or high channel counts; requires a reference image; sensitive to the choice of `eps` and `num_iters`.
-- **When to use**: When precise color matching between two images is required, especially if the transformation is non-linear or involves clipping.
-- **When not to use**: For real-time applications with limited computational resources; when no reference image is available.
+- **Time Complexity**: Each iteration involves constructing a quadratic expansion matrix ($O(N \cdot C^2)$), solving $C$ least squares problems ($O(N \cdot K^2)$ per channel, where $K \approx C^2/2$), and applying the warp ($O(N \cdot K \cdot C)$). Total time is $O(\text{num\_iters} \cdot N \cdot C^4)$.
+- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N \cdot C^2)$ to store `a_mat` and masks.
+- **Strengths**: Handles non-linear color mappings effectively. Iterative masking reduces the impact of clipped pixels, which are common in HDR or high-contrast images.
+- **Limitations**: Computationally expensive for large images due to the quadratic expansion. Sensitive to the choice of `eps`; too small a value may include noisy clipped pixels, too large a value may discard valid data.
+- **When to use**: When static color correction fails to match complex non-linear color distributions between two images, especially in medical or scientific visualization where color fidelity is critical.
+- **When not to use**: For real-time applications or very large images where performance is a concern.
 
 ## Background
-Iterative Color Matching is described in the context of the InternRobotics/AnySplat project, which appears to be an unofficial repository or tool for 3D reconstruction and visualization [S1, unofficial]. The method is used to ensure consistent color representation across different views or processed images in the pipeline.
+Iterative Color Matching is used in computer vision and graphics for color correction and style transfer. It is implemented in projects such as AnySplat and mrGSplat for initializing Gaussian splats with correct colors [S1, S2, unofficial]. The method relies on iterative least squares estimation to handle non-linearities and clipping, which are common challenges in image processing.
 
